QUESTION 4: Array Operations Using Methods
--------------------------------------------------
Write a class ArrayOps with the following STATIC methods. Do not use Arrays.sort or any other built-in utility to do the work.

  1. int[] removeDuplicates(int[] arr)
        Returns a new array with duplicates removed, keeping the first occurrence of each value.
        Example: {4, 2, 4, 7, 2, 9} -> {4, 2, 7, 9}

  2. int[] rotateLeft(int[] arr, int k)
        Returns a new array rotated left by k positions. k can be larger than the array length.
        Example: {1, 2, 3, 4, 5}, k = 2 -> {3, 4, 5, 1, 2}

  3. int secondLargest(int[] arr)
        Returns the second largest distinct value. If it does not exist, return -1.
        Example: {10, 5, 10, 8} -> 8

  4. int[] mergeSorted(int[] a, int[] b)
        Both inputs are already sorted ascending. Return one sorted array containing all elements (do this in one pass, without sorting again).
        Example: {1, 4, 7} and {2, 3, 9} -> {1, 2, 3, 4, 7, 9}

  5. boolean isPalindrome(int[] arr)
        Returns true if the array reads the same forwards and backwards.

  6. void printArray(int[] arr)
        Prints in the format [1, 2, 3]

In main(), test every method with at least two different inputs, including edge cases such as an empty array, a single element array and all elements equal.

